@startuml
title ThreadPoolExecutor — worker dispatch and shutdown

participant "OS worker thread" as Thread
participant "Dispatcher coroutine" as Dispatcher
queue "Worker service queue" as Service
queue "Local Chase-Lev deque" as Local
queue "Global incoming queue" as Incoming
participant "Peer worker deques" as Peers
participant "Notifier" as Notifier
participant "Task coroutine" as Task

Thread -> Dispatcher : start with worker TLS context
loop until stopping and accepted work is drained
  Dispatcher -> Service : try_dequeue()
  alt continuation/service task available
    Service --> Dispatcher : handle
  else no service task
    Dispatcher -> Local : pop()
    alt local task available
      Local --> Dispatcher : handle
    else no local task
      Dispatcher -> Incoming : check count; try_dequeue()
      alt incoming task available
        Incoming --> Dispatcher : handle
        Dispatcher -> Incoming : decrement count
      else no incoming task
        Dispatcher -> Peers : randomized scan; steal one handle
        Peers --> Dispatcher : handle or no work
      end
    end
  end

  alt handle found
    Dispatcher -> Task : symmetric transfer / resume
    Task --> Dispatcher : suspends or completes
  else pool is stopping and this worker has no work
    Dispatcher -> Thread : exit dispatcher
  else no work yet
    Dispatcher -> Dispatcher : bounded spin with _mm_pause
    Dispatcher -> Notifier : register sleeper and wait on generation
    Notifier --> Dispatcher : notification or predicate becomes true
    Dispatcher -> Dispatcher : atomically retire sleeper/wake reservation
  end
end

note over Dispatcher,Notifier
  Wake-ups are coalesced: an enqueue reserves a notification only for an
  unreserved sleeping worker. The packed sleep-state atomic coordinates
  sleeping-worker and outstanding-wakeup counts.
end note

note over Dispatcher,Peers
  Work stealing is one coroutine handle per successful steal. The current
  implementation scans randomized victim queues and has no batch-steal path.
end note
@enduml
